## 8. Hardware Debugging & Serial Console

To monitor the kernel boot logs and early assembly messages, connect an **FTDI USB-to-UART 3.3V serial adapter** to the Raspberry Pi 2 GPIO header:

### 8.1 Pinout Wiring

```text
Raspberry Pi 2 GPIO Header (Top Edge)
  Pin 02 [ 5V  ] [ 5V  ] Pin 04
  Pin 06 [ GND ] [ TXD ] Pin 08  -------> FTDI RXD (Yellow/Orange)
  Pin 10 [ RXD ] [ ... ] Pin 12  <------- FTDI TXD (Green/Yellow)
  Pin 06 [ GND ] ------------------------> FTDI GND (Black)
```

| RPi 2 Physical Pin | Broadcom Pin / Function | FTDI Adapter Lead |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Pin 6** | Ground (`GND`) | Ground (`GND`) |
| **Pin 8** | GPIO 14 (`UART0_TXD`) | Receive (`RXD`) |
| **Pin 10** | GPIO 15 (`UART0_RXD`) | Transmit (`TXD`) |

> [!CAUTION]
> **Never connect the 5V or 3.3V power lead** of the FTDI adapter if the Raspberry Pi is already powered via its Micro-USB power supply.

### 8.2 Serial Terminal Connection
Open a serial terminal on your host:

```bash
# Using picocom
picocom -b 115200 -d 8 -p n -f n /dev/ttyUSB0

# Or using minicom
minicom -D /dev/ttyUSB0 -b 115200
```

